{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Yesterday and the day before</b> — two times in the Masorah: here, and the other: \"unto a people you did not know, yesterday and the day before\" — Ruth came \"unto a people she did not know, yesterday and the day before.\" They juxtaposed them; therefore from her came a redeemer who would redeem them. And Israel came unto Egypt, who did not know them, \"yesterday and the day before,\" and oppressed them and enslaved them and cast their children into the river; therefore from them came Moses, who struck them with ten plagues and took their wealth and drowned them." "<b>Crying out</b> — two times in the Masorah: here, and the other in the portion of Genesis, where Pharaoh said to them: whatever is lacking from your quota of bricks, you must press yourselves into the building — and this is: \"crying out to me from the earth.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The phrase תמול שלשום appears in Deut 4:9 and Ruth 2:11; the Ba'al HaTurim reads the former as addressed to Israel regarding a people they did not know, and links both to Egypt.", 1 "The second occurrence cited is Gen 4:10; the Ba'al HaTurim connects Pharaoh's brick-quota decree to the cry of Abel's blood from the ground."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:12:01.555978010Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}